# Line endings are left alone for the C sources: stored LF, checked out LF.
#
# An earlier revision stored them CRLF on the theory that OS/2's editors need
# it. They do not need it badly enough to be worth the risk: LF did not cause
# the Open Watcom crash it was meant to fix, and shipping CRLF means any
# transfer that also converts (ASCII-mode FTP, some file shares, git with
# core.autocrlf) produces \r\r\n, which genuinely does break editors. LF is the
# neutral choice -- every tool in the chain knows how to convert it exactly
# once, and nothing converts it twice.
#
# Only the files that would actively break if a client converted them are
# pinned, so a clone with core.autocrlf=true cannot damage the build: GNU make
# treats a trailing CR as part of the recipe, and the shell and Python helpers
# are executed directly.
Makefile  text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
